A||||2||DJRice||86 2m4 Rescued - Need opinions on an front susp prob...||||5||
Z||000000||DJRice||01-31-2000||12:18 PM||||My dad needed another Fiero like he needed a hole in the head, but here's the story,  Man buys daughter a Fiero, Daughter hits a curb with Fiero, Man thinks the <B>Frame is bent</B> after dismantling most of the front passenger wheel hardware.  Man gets discouraged and sells car for parts to us for $200.<P>Im asking for opinions here because it doesnt <B>look</B> like the frame is bent. The aluminum rim is definitely roughed up. Could this girl have hit a curb hard enough to bend the frame? We got the car with the front passenger side in parts so we have no idea what the symptoms were while driving it, but is it possible that she just really screwed up the alignment and maybe bent the rim? Maybe also messed up steering rack?<P>Has anyone else bent the frame in this area?<P>Or did we just make out like a champ?<BR>  <BR>Opinions please.  <P>BTW - The guy lived in an apartment complex and couldnt leave the car up on blocks in the parking lot forever.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  It has the original red paint with crappy flaky clearcoat and a avg/below avg tan interior. (no power accessories, sorry  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">) 118K on the Auto 4 cyl that runs and shifts good.  We even got the title.  I wish we could stumble onto an 88 like this.<P>||||reg||5
Z||000001||batboy||01-31-2000||12:55 PM||||It is possible to tweak the frame from whacking the curb with a tire, but probably not bad enough to cause a big problem. Almost always the wheel is the first thing to get damaged and then the next most likely problem usually is a bent control arm(s). Obviously, the alignment is sure to be way out. A frame shop is the best place to find out if the frame is tweaked.||||reg||1
Z||000002||ChadMan||01-31-2000||03:40 PM||||I bet your frame is fine, DJ.  In my 84 Coupe, I was hit at a 90 degree angle in the left front fender (right in the wheel well) by a Plymouth Reliant, hard enough to turn my car 90 degrees to the right and destroy the upper suspension arm and bend the wheel.  That Reliant was absolutely farqed up...I mean destroyed.<P>I had my car towed to a body shop, where they diagnosed the frame as being bent only 0.75" from true when measured at the front bumper mount!  They straightened the frame, I had the suspension rebuilt, and I drove that car another 40K miles.<P>I'd bet any damage to your 86 is limited to the suspension/steering components.||||reg||1
